# What a checkout may rewrite, and what it may not.
#
# Git decides text-versus-binary by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8 KB.
# A PDF is mostly ASCII and a small one often carries no NUL at all, so the
# guess lands on "text" and a checkout with `core.autocrlf` turns every LF
# into CRLF. That shifts every byte offset in the file, and a PDF names its
# objects by offset: the cross-reference table stops matching and the reader
# rebuilds it. The same goes for a font program, an ICC profile and a fuzz
# seed, each of which is a byte string with a length its own header records.
#
# So the rule is the opposite of the guess: declare the formats binary, and
# let the text formats normalise.

* text=auto

*.pdf binary
*.in binary
*.jp2 binary
*.jbig2 binary
*.jpx binary

*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.ttc binary
*.cff binary
*.pfb binary
*.icc binary

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.mp4 binary
*.bin binary

# Fuzz seeds and corpora are raw byte strings, and most carry no extension.
fuzz/seeds/** binary
fuzz/artifacts/** binary

# Golden dumps are compared byte for byte against a tool's output, so the
# bytes have to survive a round trip through a checkout unchanged.
*.txt -text
